# How the benchmark uses the CPU cores. Read by prepare_instance.sh when it starts the daemon and by
# run_instance.sh when it runs an instance without one; an environment variable of the same name wins.
# XLA fixes it at startup, so it holds for the daemon's whole life, i.e. for one submission.
#
# CSETS_CPU_DEVICES  1 (default): one XLA CPU device; every operation of a batch is spread over all cores.
#                    N, or auto (= nproc): split the CPU into N XLA devices. A batched CPU instance may then
#                    be split into as many shards as divide its batch (at most N), one per device, run in
#                    parallel; prepare_instance.sh measures it split and unsplit and keeps the faster, since
#                    splitting only pays off for operations with enough work per repetition.
#
# There is no switch for single-threaded operations: with jax 0.11, XLA:CPU sizes its thread pool by the
# cores available to the process and ignores --xla_cpu_multi_thread_eigen for its matrix products
# (measured: 19 of 22 cores busy either way).
CSETS_CPU_DEVICES="${CSETS_CPU_DEVICES:-auto}"